    },
    {
      "id": "claim_c9409cf3b9024e7b9b553285fb52d8f1",
      "statement": "Each AWS region gets its own self-contained deployment (VPC, internal ALB, Route 53 Private Hosted Zone) that all answer to the identical service FQDN; the PHZs share the same zone name but have distinct Zone IDs and are each associated with exactly one VPC, so a VPC's Route 53 Resolver only answers from zones associated with that VPC and cross-region DNS leakage is structurally impossible.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/networking.md#L22-L36",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:867e8a18dacd339ebdb87bfe401bc3b3390eeaf2369660fbfe54c17502b9c753:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/networking.md#L80-L89",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:15033e5b0db6cdcf835b5a33808c2c1e8ae5143de5e77569ce9fdc22b4b490b9:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://terraform/regional/route53-private.tf#L1-L32",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:fe91d50c60c5371de119b344fb6e9ed7b5f31f3164c5216882b51eb33e534a5a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_deaeeae2f02040669b9a765b243e6143",
      "statement": "The public Route 53 zone for the service's internal_fqdn never contains an A record for the service name, only the ACM DNS-01 _acme-challenge CNAME, so the service FQDN is NXDOMAIN from outside any of the VPCs.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/networking.md#L86-L89",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e3afeba35ad78688a7d6fa49026a9266be6fa9633524bf65a9d4677b42637dd0:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://terraform/global/dns.tf#L1-L30",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e56ff4ac026aed367806e158c8c528d22725c3691fedb218b7c3060d896828f3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_96d04c83e6a24b339e50116c751746bf",
      "statement": "The ACM DNS-01 validation CNAME for the shared internal_fqdn is created unconditionally once by terraform/global (via a minimal, never-attached 'validation-anchor' ACM certificate and an aws_route53_record.cert_validation keyed by public_zone_id), superseding an earlier per-region manage_validation_record flag; each regional workspace then requests its own regional ACM certificate for the same internal_fqdn and waits via aws_acm_certificate_validation for ACM to report it ISSUED against the already-existing global CNAME, letting all regional applies and destroys run in parallel without racing on a shared Route 53 record.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://terraform/global/dns.tf#L1-L41",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:510e084a100e3fdd88b60725d3f854db743a7a4df93215a99d00d1f36f1ba577:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://terraform/regional/acm.tf#L1-L34",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:0c4c1286487f7750b023663ceb3ce49d3ca163ca9815cc157e61068ef45ca7c3:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_267fd45b73c9466c92dac34f98bc6a01",
      "statement": "Because the global workspace unconditionally owns the shared ACM validation CNAME (and the IAM roles/Secrets Manager secret the regional ECS tasks depend on), the global workspace must be applied at least once before any regional workspace's first apply.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://hcp-terraform/rosa-trusted-actions/main.tf",
          "version": "repo-file-v1:sha256:f31b2abcbf4cbcab0f033054620d4a977a3b0452145c1b67e7cbfed27d369a81"
